<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_match.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-match">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-match">
  <primary>MATCH</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>MATCH</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>MATCH</refname>
  <refpurpose>find every place in the graph where a pattern fits</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
[ OPTIONAL ] MATCH <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> ] ... [ WHERE <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable> ]

where <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> is:

    [ <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> = ] <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> [ <replaceable class="parameter">edge</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> ] ...

where <replaceable class="parameter">node</replaceable> is:

    ( [ <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> ] [ : <replaceable class="parameter">label</replaceable> [ ONLY ] ] [ { <replaceable class="parameter">property_map</replaceable> } ] )

where <replaceable class="parameter">edge</replaceable> is one of:

    -[ <replaceable class="parameter">edge_body</replaceable> ]-&gt;      left to right
    &lt;-[ <replaceable class="parameter">edge_body</replaceable> ]-      right to left
    -[ <replaceable class="parameter">edge_body</replaceable> ]-       either direction

where <replaceable class="parameter">edge_body</replaceable> is:

    [ <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> ] [ : <replaceable class="parameter">label</replaceable> [ ONLY ] ] [ * [ <replaceable class="parameter">min</replaceable> ] [ .. [ <replaceable class="parameter">max</replaceable> ] ] ] [ { <replaceable class="parameter">property_map</replaceable> } ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-match-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>MATCH</command> reads the graph. It emits one row for every way its pattern can be
   laid over the stored vertices and edges. Each named element of the pattern is bound to the
   vertex, the edge or the path it matched, and every later clause in the pipeline sees those
   bindings. A <command>MATCH</command> in the middle of a pipeline runs once per incoming row
   and joins its matches to that row, the way a join adds rows to a relational query.
  </para>

  <para>
   The full pattern grammar is described in <xref linkend="cypher-path"/>.
   <xref linkend="cypher-create"/> and <xref linkend="cypher-merge"/> write with the same
   syntax. The points that matter when reading are these.
  </para>

  <para>
   A pattern element with no variable is still matched. It just cannot be referred to later.
   Leave the variable out when the element is there only to constrain the shape. A label
   restricts an element to that label and to its child labels, and <literal>ONLY</literal>
   restricts it to the label itself. Without a label, a node pattern matches every vertex in
   the graph and an edge pattern matches every edge.
  </para>

  <para>
   A property map in the pattern is an equality test on each key it lists. It is shorthand:
   <literal>(m:movie {year: 1999})</literal> and
   <literal>(m:movie) WHERE m.year = 1999</literal> match the same vertices. Commas separate
   independent patterns inside one <command>MATCH</command>. Patterns that share a variable
   are joined on it, and patterns that share nothing produce their Cartesian product.
  </para>

  <para>
   Within one pattern match no edge is used twice. Node bindings may repeat, so a pattern can
   close a triangle, but a two hop pattern never doubles back along the edge it arrived on. An
   edge variable may be used once per pattern, and reusing it raises
   <literal>duplicate variable</literal>. A node variable may be repeated, which constrains
   both positions to the same vertex.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>MATCH</command> may lead a query or sit anywhere after the first clause. It never
   ends one: a query has to finish with <xref linkend="cypher-return"/>,
   <xref linkend="cypher-finish"/> or a write clause.
  </para>

  <refsect2 id="cypher-match-optional">
   <title>OPTIONAL MATCH</title>

   <para>
    <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> takes the same patterns and matches them the same way.
    The difference is what happens when a pattern finds nothing. A plain
    <command>MATCH</command> drops the row. <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> keeps it and
    binds every variable the pattern introduces to null. It is the graph counterpart of a
    <literal>LEFT JOIN</literal>.
   </para>

   <para>
    Variables already in scope keep their values. Only the variables the optional pattern
    introduces can become null. Leading a query with <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> yields
    one row of nulls when the pattern matches nothing, rather than no rows at all. Several
    comma separated patterns in one clause are optional together: either they all match, or
    the whole clause contributes nulls.
   </para>

   <para>
    A <literal>WHERE</literal> attached to an <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> is part of the
    optional match. A row whose pattern matched but whose condition failed is kept with nulls,
    not dropped. To drop it, filter after the clause with <xref linkend="cypher-with"/> or
    <xref linkend="cypher-filter"/>.
   </para>

   <para>
    Reading a property of a null element gives null rather than an error, so
    <literal>m.title</literal> is safe on a row the pattern did not match. Testing the variable
    itself, <literal>WHERE m IS NULL</literal>, is the reliable way to detect that row, because
    a matched vertex may legitimately be missing the property you would otherwise test. A
    <xref linkend="cypher-set"/> whose target element is null skips that element instead of
    failing.
   </para>
  </refsect2>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-match-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>OPTIONAL</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Keeps an input row whose pattern found nothing, with the pattern's variables bound to
      null.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      One path pattern, optionally preceded by
      <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable> and <literal>=</literal>.
      Several patterns may be listed, separated by commas. See
      <xref linkend="cypher-path"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">path_variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name bound to the whole matched path, as a value carrying its vertices and edges in
      order. Read it with <literal>length()</literal>, <literal>nodes()</literal> and
      <literal>relationships()</literal>.
     </para>
     <para>
      This position takes a narrower set of names than an element variable does. An unreserved
      keyword such as <literal>path</literal> is rejected here unless it is double quoted.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name bound to the matched vertex or edge. Optional. Visible to every clause after this
      one.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A vertex label on a node pattern, an edge label on an edge pattern. One label per
      element. Two labels separated by <literal>|</literal> are refused with
      <literal>multiple types for relationship not supported</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ONLY</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Excludes child labels, so the element matches rows of that label's own table and nothing
      that inherits from it. It goes after the label and before the property map, and before
      the <literal>*</literal> of a variable length edge. It requires a label, and without one
      raises <literal>ONLY must have one label preceding it</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">property_map</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A map literal, or a query parameter holding one. Each key is compared for equality
      against the element's property of that name.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">min</replaceable>,
     <replaceable class="parameter">max</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Bounds on the number of edges a variable length edge pattern traverses.
      <literal>*n</literal> is exactly <replaceable class="parameter">n</replaceable> hops,
      <literal>*m..n</literal> is between <replaceable class="parameter">m</replaceable> and
      <replaceable class="parameter">n</replaceable>, <literal>*m..</literal> is
      <replaceable class="parameter">m</replaceable> or more, <literal>*..n</literal> is up to
      <replaceable class="parameter">n</replaceable>, and a bare <literal>*</literal> is one or
      more. A lower bound of <literal>0</literal> includes the zero length path, which binds
      both ends of the pattern to the same vertex.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A boolean expression filtering the matches, evaluated with every variable in the pattern
      bound. See <xref linkend="cypher-where"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-match-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Find one vertex by a property. A vertex prints as
   <literal>label[id]{properties}</literal>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})
RETURN p;

                         p
---------------------------------------------------
 person[4.1]{"born": 1964, "name": "Keanu Reeves"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Follow an edge in one direction. Both endpoints carry a label, and the edge is bound to
   <literal>r</literal> so its <literal>role</literal> property can be projected:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[r:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
RETURN p.name AS actor, r.role AS role
ORDER BY actor;

        actor         |    role
----------------------+------------
 "Carrie-Anne Moss"   | "Trinity"
 "Keanu Reeves"       | "Neo"
 "Laurence Fishburne" | "Morpheus"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Leave out the elements you do not need. Here the edge has no variable and the far endpoint
   has no label. Both still constrain the match, and neither costs a binding:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})-[:directed]-&gt;(m)
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film;

      film
----------------
 "Inception"
 "Interstellar"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An edge pattern with no arrowhead matches the edge whichever way it points, and writing
   arrowheads at both ends, <literal>&lt;-[:related_to]-&gt;</literal>, means the same thing.
   <literal>Total Recall</literal> has one outgoing <literal>related_to</literal> edge and one
   incoming one:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Total Recall'})-[:related_to]-(n:movie)
RETURN n.title AS neighbour
ORDER BY neighbour;

  neighbour
--------------
 "Inception"
 "The Matrix"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Join two patterns on a shared variable. Both patterns mention <literal>m</literal>, so they
   are joined on it. Two consecutive <command>MATCH</command> clauses give the same result,
   and so does one pattern that meets in the middle,
   <literal>(a:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)&lt;-[:directed]-(d:person)</literal>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (a:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie), (d:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(m)
WHERE m.title = 'The Matrix'
RETURN a.name AS actor, d.name AS director
ORDER BY actor, director;

        actor         |     director
----------------------+-------------------
 "Carrie-Anne Moss"   | "Lana Wachowski"
 "Carrie-Anne Moss"   | "Lilly Wachowski"
 "Keanu Reeves"       | "Lana Wachowski"
 "Keanu Reeves"       | "Lilly Wachowski"
 "Laurence Fishburne" | "Lana Wachowski"
 "Laurence Fishburne" | "Lilly Wachowski"
(6 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Put <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> and <literal>=</literal> in front
   of a pattern to bind the path itself:
<programlisting>
MATCH p = (a:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})-[:related_to*1..3]-&gt;(b:movie)
RETURN length(p) AS hops, [x IN nodes(p) | x.title] AS route
ORDER BY hops, route;

 hops |                                            route
------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1    | ["The Matrix", "The Matrix Reloaded"]
 1    | ["The Matrix", "The Terminator"]
 1    | ["The Matrix", "Total Recall"]
 2    | ["The Matrix", "The Matrix Reloaded", "The Matrix Revolutions"]
 2    | ["The Matrix", "The Terminator", "Terminator 2: Judgment Day"]
 2    | ["The Matrix", "Total Recall", "Inception"]
 3    | ["The Matrix", "The Matrix Reloaded", "The Matrix Revolutions", "The Matrix Resurrections"]
(7 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>*</literal> on an edge pattern repeats it, and a fixed length behaves like the
   same pattern written out longhand. A lower bound of <literal>0</literal> includes the zero
   length path, so the starting vertex is one of the answers:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})-[:related_to*0..1]-&gt;(n:movie)
RETURN n.title AS reached
ORDER BY reached;

        reached
-----------------------
 "The Matrix"
 "The Matrix Reloaded"
 "The Terminator"
 "Total Recall"
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A label matches its child labels too, because a label is a table and a child label inherits
   from it. <literal>ONLY</literal> narrows the match to the one label. Give
   <literal>person</literal> a child label to see the difference:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL crew INHERITS (person);
CREATE (:crew {name: 'Bill Pope', born: 1952});

CREATE VLABEL
UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person) RETURN count(*) AS people;

 people
--------
 29
(1 row)
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person ONLY) RETURN count(*) AS people;

 people
--------
 28
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>ONLY</literal> works the same on an edge pattern,
   <literal>-[r:acted_in ONLY]-&gt;</literal>. Undo the setup with:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:crew) DETACH DELETE p;
DROP VLABEL crew;

UPDATE 1
DROP VLABEL
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A <literal>WHERE</literal> attached to a <command>MATCH</command> is part of the match, not
   a later step:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.year &gt; 2015
RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year
ORDER BY year, film;

            film            | year
----------------------------+------
 "Blade Runner 2049"        | 2017
 "The Matrix Resurrections" | 2021
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An edge pattern carries at most one label. Listing two is refused:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[r:acted_in|directed]-&gt;(m:movie) RETURN count(r);

ERROR:  multiple types for relationship not supported
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Match an unlabelled edge instead and test its label in <literal>WHERE</literal>.
   <literal>label()</literal> returns the element's label as a string, so
   <literal>IN</literal> over a list of names is the general form, and the only way to match a
   set of edge labels:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[r]-&gt;(m:movie)
WHERE label(r) IN ['acted_in', 'directed']
RETURN count(r) AS edges;

 edges
-------
 46
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> keeps the rows the pattern does not match. Two of these
   three people directed nothing, so <literal>m</literal> is null for them and
   <literal>m.title</literal> is null too:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.born &gt; 1974
OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
RETURN p.name AS person, m.title AS directed
ORDER BY person;

      person       | directed
-------------------+-----------
 "Neill Blomkamp"  | "Elysium"
 "Ryan Gosling"    |
 "Sam Worthington" |
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A condition inside an <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> is part of the match.
   <literal>Jurassic Park</literal> has one actor, Sam Neill, born 1947, so the film survives
   with a null actor:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.genre = 'Adventure'
OPTIONAL MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m) WHERE p.born &gt; 1950
RETURN m.title AS film, p.name AS actor
ORDER BY film, actor;

      film       | actor
-----------------+-------
 "Jurassic Park" |
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   With the same condition applied after the clause, the film is dropped:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.genre = 'Adventure'
OPTIONAL MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m)
WITH m, p WHERE p.born &gt; 1950
RETURN m.title AS film, p.name AS actor
ORDER BY film, actor;

 film | actor
------+-------
(0 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Leading a query with <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> gives one row, with
   <literal>m</literal> null. A plain <command>MATCH</command> here returns no rows at all:
<programlisting>
OPTIONAL MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Nope'})
RETURN m;

 m
---

(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>Gattaca</literal> has no outgoing <literal>related_to</literal> edge, so
   <literal>n</literal> is null and the <xref linkend="cypher-set"/> writes nothing:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Gattaca'})
OPTIONAL MATCH (m)-[:related_to]-&gt;(n:movie)
SET n.checked = true
RETURN m.title AS film, n.title AS related;

   film    | related
-----------+---------
 "Gattaca" |
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-match-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   A <command>MATCH</command> may not follow an <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> directly. A
   pattern leaves state behind for the next clause to resolve, and an
   <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> strands it on the nullable side of its join:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)
OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
MATCH (m)-[:related_to]-&gt;(x:movie)
RETURN count(*);

ERROR:  MATCH right after OPTIONAL MATCH is not allowed
HINT:  Use a WITH clause between them
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The named path variable is parsed more narrowly than an element variable.
   <literal>(path)</literal> is an acceptable node variable, but
   <literal>MATCH path = ...</literal> is a syntax error because <literal>path</literal> is a
   keyword. Write <literal>MATCH p = ...</literal>, or double quote the name.
  </para>

  <para>
   A label test belongs in <literal>label(n) = '...'</literal> and not in a colon in
   <literal>WHERE</literal>. <literal>WHERE n:movie</literal> is a syntax error, and so is a
   bare pattern used as a predicate: <literal>WHERE (m)-[:related_to]-&gt;(:movie)</literal>
   does not parse. Write
   <literal>WHERE EXISTS { MATCH (m)-[:related_to]-&gt;(:movie) }</literal> instead. See
   <xref linkend="cypher-subqueryexpressions"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   A range whose lower bound is above its upper bound is rejected at parse time with
   <literal>invalid range</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   A property map compares jsonb values, and so does a comparison against a property. That
   matters when the literal's type does not match the stored one. See
   <xref linkend="cypher-where"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-match-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>MATCH</command> implements the GQL match statement of ISO/IEC 39075:2024, section
   14.4. <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> is the optional match statement of the same
   section.
  </para>

  <para>
   The standard's label expression, section 16.8, combines label names with
   <literal>|</literal> for disjunction, <literal>&amp;</literal> for conjunction and
   <literal>!</literal> for negation. AgensGraph accepts a single label name per pattern
   element, so none of the three is available.
   <literal>-[r:acted_in|directed]-&gt;</literal> raises
   <literal>multiple types for relationship not supported</literal>, and
   <literal>-[r:acted_in|:directed]-&gt;</literal> is a syntax error. openCypher spells the
   disjunction with a pipe too, and it is refused here on a fixed length edge, on a variable
   length edge and inside <literal>shortestpath</literal> alike. Use
   <literal>WHERE label(r) IN [...]</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The standard writes a label test as <literal>n IS LABELED movie</literal> or
   <literal>n:movie</literal>, section 19.9. AgensGraph accepts neither spelling. Use
   <literal>label(n) = 'movie'</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The standard's element pattern filler, section 16.7, holds a variable declaration, a label
   expression and a predicate, and nothing else. There is no <literal>ONLY</literal>.
   AgensGraph adds it because a label here is a table that child labels inherit from, so a
   label match is a match over an inheritance tree.
  </para>

  <para>
   The standard's optional operand, section 14.4, also allows a braced or parenthesized block
   of match statements, <literal>OPTIONAL { MATCH ... MATCH ... }</literal>. AgensGraph accepts
   <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> only directly in front of <command>MATCH</command> and of
   <xref linkend="cypher-call"/>. The block form is a syntax error.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

